    "q-213282": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213282",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 5a≡3\\cdot 5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\n$$\n\nSince $3$ and {{field-1}} are {{field-2}}, this congruence {{field-3}} $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$",
      "worked_solution": "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=3$ and the modulus $n=16$ are coprime since $\\text{gcd}(3,16)=1.$ As a result, we can cancel out $3$ from both sides of the congruence:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{3}\\cdot 5a≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\,⟺\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $3$ and $16$ are $\\text{coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is equivalent to}$ $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
