    "q-86314": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86314",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\nI. $k=8$\n\nII. $k=9$\n\nIII. $k=-11$",
      "worked_solution": "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=15.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=8,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,15)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=8.$\n\n- If $k=9,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(9,15)=3\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=9.$\n\n- If $k=-11,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-11,15)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-11.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
