    "q-213163": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213163",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2a≡5\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\n$$\n\nSince $5$ and $16$ are {{field-1}}, this congruence {{field-2}} $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$",
      "worked_solution": "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=5$ and the modulus $n=16$ are coprime. As a result, we can cancel out $5$ from both sides of the congruence:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2a≡\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\,⟺\\,2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $5$ and $16$ are $\\text{coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is equivalent to}$ $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
